# BlockML xmlns prefix convention

**Rule of thumb:** short prefix = BlockML. Foreign domains use a domain token, not BlockML aliases.

If you read `core:`, `type:`, `unit:`, or `lang:` it is always `org.blockml.bml.*` — no lookup.

## BlockML (`org.blockml.bml.*`)

| Prefix | Package |
|--------|---------|
| `blockml` | `org.blockml.bml` |
| `core` | `org.blockml.bml.core` |
| `type` | `org.blockml.bml.type` |
| `unit` | `org.blockml.bml.unit` |
| `lang` | `org.blockml.bml.language` |
| `def` | `org.blockml.bml.definition` |
| `constraint` / `status` / `validation` / … | matching package segment |
| `bml` | `org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation` |
| `ann` | `org.blockml.bml.annotation` |

Do **not** use legacy `bcore` / `btype`.

Third-party libraries bind their own prefixes. They must not reuse BlockML aliases (`core`, `type`, `unit`, `lang`, …).

```xml
<!-- ❌ BAD — steals BlockML aliases -->
xmlns:core="com.example.bml.core"
xmlns:type="com.example.bml.type"

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type"
xmlns:excore="com.example.bml.core"
xmlns:extype="com.example.bml.type"
```

See also `blockml-xmlns-ordering.mdc` (attribute order).
